{0 World and components}

{!Eon_engine.World} wraps {!Eon_ecs.World} with a phantom-typed capability
handle and a typed component-descriptor API in place of raw strings.
Examples below are from the companion
{{:https://github.com/imunitic/eon/blob/main/eon_engine/examples/book/world_and_components_example.ml}[world_and_components_example.ml]}.

{1 World capabilities}

{!Eon_engine.World} carries a phantom type parameter — ['perm World.t] —
that enforces read/write discipline at compile time:

- [rw World.t] — full access; returned by [World.create] and held by the loop
- [ro World.t] — read-only view; obtained via [World.readonly]; passed to [Parallel] system updates

Write operations ([create_entity], [add_component], [set_component], …) are
typed to accept only [rw World.t]. Read operations accept either:

{[
  let world : World.rw World.t = make_world () in
  let player = World.create_entity world in

  let ro : World.ro World.t = World.readonly world in
  assert (World.is_alive ro player);

  (* World.create_entity ro would be a type error — ro has no write ops.
     Only rw handles reach this line: *)
  let _second = World.create_entity world in
  ()
]}

[readonly]/[as_ro] are zero-cost — only the phantom type changes, nothing
is copied.

{1 Component descriptors}

Components are identified by a typed descriptor value rather than raw
strings — the descriptor carries the value type as a phantom parameter,
eliminating the [Obj.magic] a string-keyed API would otherwise need at the
boundary:

{[
  let health : int Components.t = Eon_engine.component "Health"

  let world = make_world () in
  let _ = World.register world health in
  let player = World.create_entity world in

  World.add_component world player health 100;
  match World.get_component world player health with
  | Some hp -> assert (hp = 100)
  | None -> assert false
]}

{1 Built-in components}

{!Eon_engine.Components} ships typed component modules for common
engine-level data:

{table
  {tr {th Module} {th Type} {th Description}}
  {tr {td [Components.Local_transform]}  {td [{position; rotation; scale}]} {td Transform relative to parent (or world origin)}}
  {tr {td [Components.World_transform]}  {td [{position; rotation; scale}]} {td Fully composed world-space transform — read-only}}
  {tr {td [Components.Parent]}           {td [{entity : entity_id}]}        {td Parent entity reference; absence = root}}
  {tr {td [Components.Children]}         {td [{entities : entity_id list}]}  {td Engine-maintained child entity list}}
  {tr {td [Components.Velocity]}         {td [{dx: float; dy: float}]}       {td 2D velocity}}
  {tr {td [Components.Sprite]}           {td [Sprite.t]}                     {td Rendering info}}
  {tr {td [Components.Animation]}        {td [Animation.t]}                  {td Sprite animation}}
  {tr {td [Components.Camera]}           {td [Camera.t]}                     {td Camera projection}}
  {tr {td [Components.Collider]}         {td [Collider.t]}                   {td Collision shape}}
  {tr {td [Components.Tag]}              {td [{value: string}]}              {td String marker}}
}

Register all of them at once with [Components.Engine_components.register_all world].
Extend with game-specific components by including that module and adding
your own descriptors:

{[
  module Game_components = struct
    include Components.Engine_components

    let mana : int Components.t = Eon_engine.component "Mana"

    let register_all world =
      Components.Engine_components.register_all world;
      ignore (World.register world mana)
  end

  let world = World.create () in
  Game_components.register_all world;
  let player = World.create_entity world in
  World.add_component world player Components.Local_transform.component
    ({ position = Math.Vec2.zero; rotation = 0.0; scale = Math.Vec2.one }
     : Components.Local_transform.t);
  World.add_component world player Game_components.mana 50
]}

{1 Resources and services}

{!Eon_engine.World.add_data}/[get_data]/[add_service]/[get_service] are the
raw data and service plane inherited from [eon_ecs] — arbitrary values
keyed by open polymorphic variants. That raw plane works, but every caller
has to agree on a key by hand. {!Eon_engine.Resource} and
{!Eon_engine.Service} are typed facades over exactly that plane, generated
per-resource by a functor so the key never leaks outside the module that
owns it:

{table
  {tr {th} {th Resource}   {th Service}}
  {tr {td Cadence}         {td Written every frame or simulation tick}     {td Registered once at startup, never mutated by game systems}}
  {tr {td Write function}  {td [store] — any [Exclusive] system, or the loop} {td [register] — startup code, before the loop starts}}
  {tr {td Example}         {td {!Eon_engine.Time}, [Raw_input_frame], [Audio_command_buffer]} {td A physics-world handle, a network session, a Steam API}}
}

{[
  (* Name the record type at the TOP LEVEL, not inline inside the functor
     argument — Resource.Make's result makes Physics_state.t
     transparently equal to it, but a record type declared only inside
     an anonymous functor-argument struct isn't in scope for field-label
     disambiguation afterwards. *)
  type physics_state = { gravity : float; bodies : int }

  module Physics_state = Resource.Make (struct
    type t = physics_state
    let key = Resource.key `Physics_state
  end)

  let world = make_world () in
  Physics_state.store world { gravity = 9.8; bodies = 0 };   (* requires rw *)

  let read_gravity (world : [> World.ro ] World.t) =          (* any 'perm *)
    (Physics_state.fetch world).gravity
  in
  assert (read_gravity world = 9.8)
]}

{!Eon_engine.Time} is the concrete in-tree example: [type t = { delta;
elapsed; frame }] satisfying [Resource.S], with [write] doing the
fetch-or-zero/increment/store dance once per frame so systems never see a
missing resource after the first tick. See
[docs/design/resource_service_design.md] (ecs-032) for the full design
rationale, including why the key type is [Obj.t] internally and why that's
still type-safe at every call site.

{1 Namespace: cross-world access}

{!Eon_engine.Namespace} is a flat directory of named worlds — useful when
one system legitimately needs to reach a resource or service that lives in
a {i different} world than the one it was handed (a server's per-zone
worlds reaching a shared global world, for instance). It composes directly
with [Resource.fetch]/[Service.fetch]:

{[
  let global_world = make_world () in
  Physics_state.store global_world { gravity = 9.8; bodies = 0 };

  let ns = Namespace.create () in
  Namespace.attach ns "global" global_world;

  (* Some other system, holding a different world entirely, reaches the
     global world's resource through the namespace: *)
  let gravity = (Physics_state.fetch (Namespace.named "global" ns)).gravity in
  assert (gravity = 9.8);

  match Namespace.named "does_not_exist" ns with
  | _ -> assert false
  | exception Namespace.Unknown_namespace _ -> ()
]}

[Namespace.t] is a plain value — create it, populate it at startup
(typically via [attach] for every world that should be reachable), and
keep it wherever makes sense for your architecture (a module-level value,
stored as a service, captured in a closure). The engine doesn't prescribe
where it lives.

